course: dictionary
lesson: 6867
title: What is an overdraft?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A bank account holds your money, and usually you can only spend what is in it. An overdraft is when the bank lets you spend more than you have, so your balance drops below zero. «An overdraft lets you spend below zero, which is borrowing, and banks charge high interest on it.»
explain: Think of a number line. If you have £50 and spend £80, your balance is −£30. The minus sign means you owe the bank £30. That money is borrowed, so it is not really yours.
explain: Borrowing is not free. The bank may charge interest, which is extra money on top of what you owe, and fees as well. An overdraft can cost much more than you expected.
fact: An overdraft is when a bank lets you take out more money than you have in your account.
fact: When you are overdrawn, your balance is below zero, and it is shown with a minus sign.
fact: An overdraft also means the amount of money you owe the bank in this way.
fact: Banks often charge interest and fees on an overdraft, so you pay back more than you spent.
fact: You may hear «go into an overdraft», «pay off the overdraft» and «an overdraft limit», which is the most the bank will let you owe.
exercise: What does an overdraft let you do? | Get a free gift from the bank | Pay for things with coins only | *Spend more than you have in your account | Put money into a savings jar
exercise: Mia has £50 and spends £80. What is her balance? | £130 | *−£30 | £80 | £30
exercise: If you owe the bank £30 on your overdraft, what does that mean? | You have £30 saved in the bank | The bank has given you £30 as a present | You spent exactly the money you had | *You have borrowed £30 and must pay it back
test: Why does an overdraft cost extra? | Because you must pay in coins | Because overdrafts are always free | *Because the bank charges interest and fees on the money you borrowed | Because the bank gives you a prize for borrowing
test: Which phrase means you owe the bank money because you spent more than you had? | Win a prize | *Be overdrawn | Save up in a jar | Count your change
exam: What is an overdraft? | *Borrowing from the bank by spending more than you have | Coins you find in the street | Money a shop gives back when you return something | Keeping money safe in a piggy bank
